content.text:
Much simpler than the Claude Code case: **Codex speaks the OpenAI API, and LiteLLM serves an OpenAI-compatible API, so Codex talks to your LiteLLM proxy directly — no translation shim.** You just add a custom provider to `~/.codex/config.toml`.

From your configs: LiteLLM is on `:14000` (master-key auth), exposing `gemma4:31b`, `gemma4:26b`, `gemma3:4b`. Your Codex module already generates `config.toml` via `pkgs.formats.toml`, so this drops right in.

## What the config needs
```toml
[model_providers.litellm]
name = "LiteLLM (local Ollama)"
base_url = "http://localhost:14000/v1"
env_key = "LITELLM_API_KEY"
wire_api = "chat"

[profiles.local]
model = "gemma4:31b"
model_provider = "litellm"
```
Then run `codex --profile local` (leaving your default Codex/OpenAI setup untouched).

## Two gotchas that will bite you
1. **`wire_api = "chat"`** — Codex defaults to OpenAI's *Responses* API. LiteLLM+Ollama want **Chat Completions**; without this line Codex hits `/v1/responses` and fails. This is the single most important setting.
2. **The API key** — your LiteLLM has a master key, so Codex must authenticate. `env_key` names the env var Codex reads, so you need `export LITELLM_API_KEY=<key>`. Cleanest is to generate a **virtual key** in the LiteLLM admin UI and use that (the master key is a root-only sops secret at `mode 0400`, so the `gotha` user can't read it directly).

## In your Nix module
Extend the attrset in `home-manager/codex/default.nix` (it currently only sets `mcp_servers`):
```nix
  home.file.".codex/config.toml".source = tomlFormat.generate "codex-config.toml" {
    mcp_servers = mcpServers;

    model_providers.litellm = {
      name = "LiteLLM (local Ollama)";
      base_url = "http://localhost:14000/v1";
      env_key = "LITELLM_API_KEY";
      wire_api = "chat";
    };

    profiles.local = {
      model = "gemma4:31b";
      model_provider = "litellm";
    };
  };
```

## Honest caveat (same as the Claude Code answer)
Your LiteLLM only fronts **Gemma** models, which have weak-to-no native function-calling. Codex drives edits through tool calls (`apply_patch`/shell) — so plain Q&A will work, but the *agentic* edit-a-file loop will likely be unreliable. For real Codex-style work you'd want a strong tool-calling coder model (e.g. add a `qwen2.5-coder` entry to your LiteLLM `model_list`). Note also your LiteLLM has `drop_params: true`, which silently strips params Ollama rejects — helpful, but it can mask capability mismatches.

Want me to wire this into the Codex module? I'd add the provider + `local` profile, and for the key either add a user-readable sops secret that exports `LITELLM_API_KEY`, or just leave it to a shell export — your call on which. I'd also suggest adding a `qwen2.5-coder` model to `litellm.nix` at the same time so the profile is actually usable for coding.

